# Debugging with VS Code & Breakpoints

## How Breakpoints Help Compared to Console Logs

Console logs are useful and I use them quite often when I want to quickly check the value of something. They are simple and usually enough for small problems.

However, breakpoints allow me to investigate the application in more detail. Instead of printing several values and trying to understand what happened afterwards, I can pause the application at a specific line and inspect its current state.

For example, if a request goes through a controller and then a service, I can stop the execution in either place and look at the variables at that exact moment.

I can also continue the program one line at a time, which makes it easier to understand where something starts going wrong.

I think console logs are still useful for quick debugging, but breakpoints are much better when I need to understand the flow of a more complicated piece of code.

## What Is `launch.json`?

`launch.json` is a configuration file used by VS Code to tell the debugger how to start an application.

Instead of simply running the application normally, the debugger needs to know things like what program should be launched and how it should be executed.

For a NestJS application, this configuration allows VS Code to start the application through the Node.js debugger so that breakpoints can be used.

